Edition No. 34 — Wednesday, 4 November 2026

10 stories: 'Overwhelmingly biased': the EU observers' final report hands Zambia 13 recommendations, and the government replies that observers cannot see what the police can (lead); A day before the declaration, Kasonde Mwenda asks the High Court to halt the Mandevu process, arguing the Commission had no power to scrap the August poll and order a new one; Seven days to answer: M'membe asks the Law Association to defend the two-term limit, call a special general meeting and say whether tenure can change without a referendum; Sixty-forty and US$213 million over two phases: the IDC and a Beijing firm sign the ZAMVAX joint venture, starting with 20 million cholera doses a year filled in Lusaka South; Oversubscribed by K3.5 billion: the Bank of Zambia formally closes the K5 billion drought and load-shedding facility, and says borrowers must still get the benefit of any rate cut; A spirit-like smell at Kafue: the ZRA stops three trucks carrying 119,500 litres of ethanol declared at Chirundu as paraffin, and says the false entry would have cost K5.8 million; The witness waits: Archbishop Banda's trial is put off to Friday 6 November because the prosecutor who was to cross-examine is before the Solwezi High Court, 700 kilometres away; Two sedition files, one Monday: Nakacinda is put on his defence over the 'genocide' maize remarks, and surety Mulenga Fube must pay K100,000 or serve six months over Binwell Mpundu; Four from four, by a point: Munali Suns hold off a Harare Kings fourth-quarter comeback to win 70–69 at NASDEC and sit alone at the top of Group E; An uncle in WITCH and a 16-track answer: Sampa the Great's 'Nu Zamrock' takes the psychedelic rock of 1970s Zambia and rebuilds it with Mag44, Sia and a family interview.

